      <a href="#which-tub">Which Tub to Buy</a>
      <a href="#plant-and-other">Plant Powder and Other Aldi Protein</a>
      <a href="#mistakes">Common Mistakes</a>
      <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
    </TableOfContents>

    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="lede">
          A 2 lb tub of Aldi's Elevation whey holds roughly 18 to 21 servings, and the newer 24 oz tub holds roughly 14 to 15. That spread is not sloppiness: Elevation's serving is unusually large, reported at 44 to 49 g of powder for 30 g of protein, and both the tub size and the serving weight have shifted between versions. This guide works through the numbers transparently so you can check them against the tub in your kitchen, then turns servings into days, weeks and the protein you actually need.
        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. 1. The Short Answer: 14 to 21 Servings
          </h2>

          <p>
            Aldi sells protein powder under its Elevation by Millville label, and the whey version has come in two sizes. The long-running 2 lb (32 oz) tub works out to roughly 18 to 21 servings. The 24 oz tub that appears in newer aldi.us listings works out to roughly 14 to 15. Both give 30 g of protein per serving per the label at the time of writing, so the difference is simply how much powder is in the tub.
          </p>

          <p>
            Those ranges come from dividing the net weight by the serving weight. A 2 lb tub holds about 907 g of powder, and Elevation's serving has been reported at 44 to 49 g depending on the flavor and the formula version. Divide 907 by 49 and you get about 18.5 servings; divide by 44 and you get about 20.6. The label itself will print a single figure, usually prefixed with the word "about", which is the manufacturer doing the same sum.
          </p>

          <p>
            The 24 oz tub is about 680 g. At 49 g per serving that is roughly 13.9 servings, and at 44 g it is roughly 15.5. In practice you should expect to get 14 servings, and occasionally a fifteenth if your scoops run light. That is about a quarter fewer servings than the 2 lb tub, which matters more than most shoppers notice, because the two tubs have been sold at very similar shelf prices.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you want one number to remember: one Elevation tub is two to three weeks of daily shakes. The big tub stretches to three weeks, the small one covers two. Everything below is detail on how to make that estimate accurate for your own tub, your own scoop habits and your own protein target, and on why the figure printed on the label is a guide rather than a guarantee.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Elevation Whey by the Numbers</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">18-21</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Servings in the 2 lb tub (est.)</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">14-15</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Servings in the 24 oz tub (est.)</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">30 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Protein per serving (label)</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">44-49 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Powder per serving (reported)</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            For the full picture on whether Elevation is worth buying in the first place, and the closest alternatives when your store is out, see our guide to the <a href="/best-aldi-protein-powder/">best Aldi protein powder</a>. This page sticks to the sizing question: how much is in the tub and how far it goes.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="two-tubs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. 2. The 2 lb Tub vs the 24 oz Tub
          </h2>

          <p>
            Elevation whey was sold for years in a 2 lb tub, and most of the reviews, nutrition database entries and forum threads you will find refer to that size. When we checked aldi.us in early November 2026, the newest listings described a 24 oz tub, while an older 32 oz vanilla listing still appeared but was out of stock. We cannot confirm whether every store has switched, so the honest advice is to read the net weight on the tub in front of you.
          </p>

          <p>
            The two tubs can look almost identical on the shelf. Aldi's packaging changes tend to be subtle, and an 8 oz drop in contents does not always mean a visibly smaller container. The net weight is printed on the front label in both ounces and grams, and the servings per container figure is on the nutrition panel at the back. Those two numbers settle the question in seconds.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Tub</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Net weight</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Servings (est.)</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Protein per tub (est.)</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Elevation whey, 2 lb</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">32 oz / about 907 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 18-21</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 540-630 g</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Elevation whey, 24 oz</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">24 oz / about 680 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 14-15</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 420-450 g</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Elevation plant, 18 oz</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">18 oz / about 510 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Not verified</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Not verified</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The protein-per-tub column is the one that matters most. Multiply 30 g of protein by the number of servings and the 2 lb tub delivers roughly 540 to 630 g of protein, while the 24 oz tub delivers roughly 420 to 450 g. That is the amount of protein you are actually buying, and it is what you should compare between tubs and between brands rather than the size of the container or the shelf price.
          </p>

          <p>
            Figures in the table are estimates from the reported serving weights and the stated net weights, not a quote from a specific label. Aldi's own listings did not show a full nutrition panel for every version, and the serving size has been reported slightly differently by different sources. Treat the ranges as a sanity check, and trust the panel on your own tub if it disagrees.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Read the net weight before you count servings.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A 2 lb Elevation tub and a 24 oz one can sit side by side at almost the same price. One holds about 18 to 21 servings, the other about 14 to 15. The front label tells you which you are holding.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="one-serving" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. 3. What One Elevation Serving Actually Is
          </h2>

          <p>
            Elevation's serving is big. Per the labels reported by reviewers and nutrition databases, it is 44 to 49 g of powder for 30 g of protein, at roughly 170 to 180 calories with about 2 g of sugar. Many mainstream wheys use a single scoop of around 30 to 33 g for 24 or 25 g of protein. So an Elevation serving is closer to one and a half times the powder of a typical scoop, which is the main reason a tub runs out faster than its size suggests.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some Elevation tubs describe the serving as two scoops rather than one. That is the single most common source of confusion, because people who use one scoop and assume it is a full serving are getting about 15 g of protein and roughly 85 to 90 calories per shake. Their tub then lasts twice as long as the label says, and they are getting half the protein they think they are. Check the wording on the panel: "2 scoops" and "1 scoop" mean very different things.
          </p>

          <p>
            The serving is heavy partly because the powder is not pure protein. Thirty grams of protein in roughly 44 to 49 g of powder means protein makes up somewhere around 60 to 68 percent of the scoop. The rest is carbohydrate, flavoring, sweeteners, thickeners and a little fat. That is typical for a sweet, mainstream concentrate and isolate blend, and it is why an unflavored isolate can deliver the same 30 g of protein in noticeably less powder.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you want to know exactly how many servings your tub holds, weigh a serving. Put a glass on a kitchen scale, zero it, add what you consider one serving, and note the weight. Then divide the net weight of the tub in grams by that number. It takes two minutes and it is more accurate than any estimate on a website, including this one, because it accounts for how you actually scoop.
          </p>

          <p>
            Scoops are not precise instruments. A heaped scoop can weigh 15 to 20 percent more than a level one, and powder that has settled in transit packs more densely than powder that has just been stirred. Labels assume a level scoop. If you habitually heap, plan on one or two fewer servings than the label claims; if you level with a knife, expect to land on the label figure or slightly above it.
          </p>

          <p>
            One more detail worth knowing: older Elevation tubs advertised added amino acids and creatine, and reviewers report that the formula later changed. If your tub lists added amino acids, the serving weight and the protein figure may differ from the current version, so the servings count on that tub's panel is the one to use. Our piece asking <a href="/is-elevation-protein-powder-good/">is Elevation protein powder good</a> goes through the label line by line.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-long" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. 4. How Long a Tub Lasts
          </h2>

          <p>
            Servings are an abstract number until you turn them into days. The table below assumes full 30 g servings and the estimated ranges above. If you drink one shake a day, a 2 lb tub lasts roughly 18 to 21 days, a little under three weeks, and a 24 oz tub lasts about two weeks. Two shakes a day halves both figures.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Shakes per day</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">2 lb tub lasts</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">24 oz tub lasts</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Protein from shakes</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Half a serving (1 scoop) a day</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 36-42 days</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 28-30 days</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 15 g a day</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">One serving a day</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 18-21 days</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 14-15 days</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">30 g a day</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">One serving, training days only (4 a week)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 4.5-5 weeks</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 3.5 weeks</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">30 g per session</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Two servings a day</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 9-10 days</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 7 days</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">60 g a day</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The training-days-only row is the one many people underestimate. If you only drink a shake after a workout, four times a week, a 2 lb tub lasts well over a month and a 24 oz tub lasts three and a half weeks. That changes the shopping maths: you are visiting Aldi once a month for protein rather than every fortnight, and stock gaps at your store matter less because you can buy ahead.
          </p>

          <p>
            Half servings are a legitimate way to use Elevation, not a cheat. One scoop of a two-scoop serving gives about 15 g of protein, which is a reasonable top-up in oatmeal, yogurt or coffee, or a lighter shake for someone with a smaller protein gap. It doubles the number of uses from the tub. Just be honest with yourself about the protein you are getting, because 15 g is not the 30 g printed in large type on the front.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are buying for a household, add the users together. Two people each having one shake a day will finish a 2 lb tub in nine or ten days and a 24 oz tub in a week. At that rate it is worth buying two or three tubs when you find them in stock, provided the best-by dates are comfortably in the future. Unopened whey stored cool and dry keeps for many months.
          </p>

          <p>
            Once opened, a tub is best finished within a few months for flavor and mixability, though a well-sealed tub usually keeps past that if it still smells and looks normal. Keep the lid tight, keep the scoop dry and keep the tub out of a humid bathroom or a hot car. Damp powder clumps, and clumped powder is much harder to scoop accurately, which quietly throws your servings count off too.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="servings-you-need" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. 5. How Many Servings You Actually Need
          </h2>

          <p>
            The right number of servings per day is not a fixed rule. It is the gap between the protein you need and the protein you already eat. A shake is a convenient way to close that gap, but if your meals already cover your target, the tub is a luxury rather than a necessity. Start by estimating your daily target, then subtract what your food provides.
          </p>

          <p>
            For healthy adults the baseline recommended intake is 0.8 g of protein per kilogram of body weight per day. Research on people who lift weights, summarized in position stands such as the International Society of Sports Nutrition's, generally supports around 1.4 to 2.0 g per kilogram for building and keeping muscle. Our guide on <a href="/how-much-protein-do-i-need/">how much protein you need</a> walks through the ranges, and the <a href="/protein-calculator/">protein calculator</a> gives you a personal number.
          </p>

          <p>
            Take a worked example. A person weighing 80 kg who trains regularly might aim for about 1.6 g per kilogram, or roughly 128 g a day. If their meals provide around 100 g, the gap is about 28 g, which one Elevation serving covers almost exactly. If their meals provide 70 g, the gap is nearly 60 g, and two servings a day, or one serving plus a higher-protein meal, would make sense.
          </p>

          <p>
            Most people land on one serving a day, and that is where Elevation's 30 g dose is genuinely useful. It is enough to cover a typical protein shortfall in one shake, and research suggests that 20 to 40 g of protein in a sitting is a sensible amount for muscle repair in most adults. Splitting your intake across meals tends to work better than one very large dose, so two smaller shakes are fine if that suits your day.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not chase servings for their own sake. Extra protein beyond your needs does not build extra muscle; it is used for energy, and it brings the calories with it. Two Elevation servings a day add roughly 340 to 360 calories, which is a meaningful amount for someone trying to lose weight. A health note, briefly: this is general information, not medical advice, and anyone with kidney disease, who is pregnant, has a milk allergy or takes regular medication should agree a protein target with a doctor or registered dietitian before adding daily shakes.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="which-tub" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. 6. Which Tub Size Should You Buy?
          </h2>

          <p>
            If your store has both, buy the 2 lb tub. It holds roughly a third more powder than the 24 oz tub, which works out to about four to six more servings, and the two have been sold at very similar shelf prices. You get more shakes per trip, fewer trips to check stock, and more protein for your money. There is no real argument for the small tub when the large one is on the shelf.
          </p>

          <p>
            The 24 oz tub makes sense in a few situations. If you are trying Elevation for the first time and are not sure about the flavor, the smaller commitment is reasonable, and Aldi's return guarantee covers you either way. If you only use protein powder occasionally, a smaller tub is more likely to be finished before it goes stale. And if the 24 oz tub is the only one your store stocks, it is still a perfectly good buy.
          </p>

          <p>
            On cost, briefly: at around $20, the 2 lb tub works out to roughly $0.95 to $1.10 per serving, while a 24 oz tub at $19.79 works out to roughly $1.30 to $1.40 per serving, using the servings estimates above. Shelf prices vary by store and Aldi's online prices can be higher than the tag. Our sister guide on <a href="/how-much-is-aldi-protein-powder/">how much Aldi protein powder costs</a> does the full price-per-gram comparison against food and name brands.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you get through a tub every couple of weeks, it is worth comparing Elevation against bulk options too. A warehouse-club whey or a 5 lb tub ordered online can hold three to four times the servings of a 24 oz Elevation tub. Our head-to-head of <a href="/aldi-vs-costco-protein-powder/">Aldi vs Costco protein powder</a> looks at how Elevation stacks up against Kirkland whey for people who buy in volume.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whatever size you buy, count it in servings and days rather than ounces. Ask yourself how many shakes you drink a week, multiply by the weeks between Aldi trips, and buy enough tubs to cover it. That simple habit stops you running out mid-week and making an expensive emergency purchase of a name-brand tub at a convenience store or pharmacy.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="plant-and-other" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. 7. The Plant Powder and Other Aldi Protein Products
          </h2>

          <p>
            Aldi has also listed an Elevation plant protein powder in chocolate and vanilla, in an 18 oz tub, or roughly 510 g. Both listings we found were out of stock when we checked, and Aldi's page did not show a nutrition panel, so we cannot tell you how many servings it holds or how much protein each one gives. Third-party sites describe it as a pea and brown rice blend, but we could not verify the serving size from Aldi itself.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you find it, the method is the same as for whey. Read the net weight in grams and the serving weight on the nutrition panel, and divide. Purely as an illustration of the arithmetic, if the label listed a 35 g serving, an 18 oz tub would hold about 14 to 15 servings; a 40 g serving would give about 12 to 13. Those are not the product's real figures, just a reminder that a smaller tub with a heavier scoop runs out quickly.
          </p>

          <p>
            Plant powders often deliver less protein per serving than whey, so check the protein figure alongside the servings count. A tub with 15 servings of 20 g of protein holds 300 g of protein in total, which is about half of what a 2 lb Elevation whey tub provides. If you need a dependable dairy-free option rather than an occasional Aldi Find, our guide to the <a href="/best-plant-based-protein-powder/">best plant-based protein powder</a> ranks the widely available alternatives.
          </p>

          <p>
            Aldi's ready-to-drink Elevation shakes are simpler to count, because each bottle or carton is one serving with the protein printed on the front. They cost more per gram of protein than a tub of powder, but there is no scooping, no shaker bottle and no guesswork. Aldi has also sold a vanilla collagen powder at times. Collagen is not a complete protein, so do not count its servings toward a muscle-building target the way you would whey.
          </p>

          <p>
            The common thread is that Aldi's protein range rotates. Sizes, formulas and flavors change, sometimes without much fanfare. The servings figures on this page are estimates built from the label information available at the time of writing. The tub in your cupboard is the final authority: net weight on the front, servings and serving weight on the back, and a quick weigh on a kitchen scale if you want certainty.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using one scoop and calling it a serving</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Some Elevation tubs define a serving as two scoops. One scoop then gives about 15 g of protein, not 30 g, and the tub appears to last twice as long as it should. Read the serving description on the nutrition panel and match your scoops to it, or weigh the powder.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming every tub holds the same number of servings</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The 2 lb tub holds roughly 18 to 21 servings and the 24 oz tub roughly 14 to 15, and the two can look alike on the shelf. Check the net weight on the front of the tub before you plan how many to buy or compare it with another brand.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Heaping the scoop every time</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A heaped scoop can weigh 15 to 20 percent more than a level one. Over a tub, that can cost you two or three servings and add calories you did not plan for. Level the scoop with the back of a knife, or weigh a serving once to learn what a level scoop looks like.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying for one when two people share the tub</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Two people drinking one shake a day each will finish a 2 lb tub in about nine or ten days. Count the household's shakes per week before you shop, and buy enough tubs to last until your next Aldi trip so you are not caught short.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating the label count as exact</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The servings figure on a tub is an approximation, usually prefixed with the word about. Settling, humidity and scoop technique all shift the real number. Use it as a planning guide, and if it matters to you, divide the net weight by a weighed serving for your own figure.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="faq-list">
            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>How many servings are in Aldi protein powder?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  It depends on the tub. A 2 lb tub of Elevation whey holds roughly 18 to 21 servings, and the newer 24 oz tub holds roughly 14 to 15, based on the reported 44 to 49 g serving. Each serving gives 30 g of protein per the label at the time of writing. Check the servings per container on your own tub, because sizes and formulas have changed over time.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>How many scoops are in an Elevation protein serving?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Some Elevation tubs describe one serving as two scoops, while others list it by weight. The reported serving is 44 to 49 g of powder for 30 g of protein. If your tub says two scoops, one scoop gives roughly 15 g of protein. Read the serving description on your nutrition panel rather than assuming one scoop is a full serving.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>How long does a tub of Aldi protein powder last?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  At one full serving a day, a 2 lb Elevation tub lasts about 18 to 21 days and a 24 oz tub about 14 to 15 days. At two servings a day, halve those figures. If you only drink a shake on four training days a week, a 2 lb tub stretches to well over a month. Half servings double the number of uses.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>How many grams of protein are in a whole Elevation tub?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Multiply 30 g by the servings count. A 2 lb tub delivers roughly 540 to 630 g of protein and a 24 oz tub roughly 420 to 450 g, using the estimated servings ranges. That total is the best figure for comparing Elevation tubs with each other and with other brands, since container sizes and scoop weights differ so widely.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Why is the Elevation serving so large?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  The powder is a sweet concentrate and isolate blend rather than pure protein. Thirty grams of protein in about 44 to 49 g of powder means protein makes up roughly 60 to 68 percent of the scoop, with carbohydrate, flavoring, sweeteners and thickeners making up the rest. Leaner isolates deliver similar protein in less powder, so their tubs hold more servings per pound.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Should I buy the 2 lb or the 24 oz Aldi protein tub?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Buy the 2 lb tub if your store has it. It holds about four to six more servings than the 24 oz tub, and the two have been sold at similar prices, so it gives you more protein for your money. The 24 oz tub is reasonable for a first try, for occasional use, or when it is the only size on the shelf.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>How many servings of protein powder should I have a day?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Most people need one at most. Work out your daily protein target, subtract what your meals provide, and use a shake to cover the gap. For many active adults one 30 g Elevation serving does it. Extra protein does not build extra muscle. People with kidney disease, pregnancy, allergies or regular medication should ask a doctor or registered dietitian first.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

          </div>
        </div>

